<lesson id="les-l11-04-name-service" title="The name service of the world" level="11" minutes="12" goal="A laptop in country B finds www.world.lab through the root name server in country A and opens the page in country C"/>
<step n="1" text="Three countries, one name book. ResidentCountryB asks WorldNameRoot in country A for every name it meets. Run nslookup www.world.lab on the laptop: the root knows root.world.lab, but not the site."/>
<step n="2" text="On WorldNameRoot, open the DNS service and add the A record: www.world.lab leads to 10.30.13.10, the server WorldSite in country C."/>
<step n="3" text="Ask again from the laptop. The question crosses one sea, the answer names a server across another." check="c1"/>
<step n="4" text="Now open http://www.world.lab/ from the laptop: name from country A, page from country C." check="c2"/>
<step n="5" text="The page was reachable all along; only its name was missing." check="c3"/>
<check id="c1" kind="nslookup" from="laptopb" to="www.world.lab" url="" expect="10.30.13.10" label="The root resolves www.world.lab to WorldSite"/>
<check id="c2" kind="http" from="laptopb" to="" url="http://www.world.lab/" expect="WorldSite" label="The page opens by its world name"/>
<check id="c3" kind="ping" from="laptopb" to="situs0" url="" expect="ok" label="ResidentCountryB reaches WorldSite by address"/>
